86045417] [17592186544659 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "143347e7-6d5a-5881-950d-01ac371e0e93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544785 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544785 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544785 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544785 #edb/ref 1071 "Gerry, a gardener, is planting roses. He knows he needs $1$ cup of water for every $4$ seeds he plants. So, Gerry creates a ratio table to determine the ratio of water to seeds for different quantities.\n\n| Water (cups) | Seeds |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ |\n| $7$ | $?$ |\n| $12$ | $48$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nHow many seeds can Gerry plant with $7$ cups of water?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544785 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07105131-b758-5e72-bdb4-02c98035c4fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544785 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio $1:4$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the number of cups of water ($1$), and the denominator represents the number of seeds ($4$).\n\nWe can make a numerator of $7$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $7$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1\\times 7}{4\\times 7} & =\\frac{7}{28}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our ratio table:\n\n| Water (cups) | Seeds |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ |\n| $7$ | $28$ |\n| $12$ | $48$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, Gerry can plant $28$ seeds with $7$ cups of water." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544885 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544885 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544885 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544885 #edb/ref 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n| $?$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $15$ |\n| $6$ | $?$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544885 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d91b69e3-fd77-5457-a2ba-3ce70f9e9f93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544885 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio $3:15$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{15}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($3$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($15$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a numerator of $6,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 2}{15\\times 2}=\\frac{6}{30}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $10,$ we divide the numerator and denominator of the previous fraction by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{6÷3}{30÷3}=\\frac{2}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| $2$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $15$ |\n| $6$ | $30$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $2$ and $30.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544960 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544960 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2e36d57-8213-53e5-bff9-ed407c12c943" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544960 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544970 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544970 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c2a4f642-7f5e-5258-bf8b-7514a82eded7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544970 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544993 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fcd7a88c-39ee-5167-897e-fa978522f159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544993 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544885 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
